<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Fenwick (Quaker)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1618">1618</a>–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as John Fenwick (colonist)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Fenwick was an English Quaker and former Parliamentarian soldier who bought Lord Berkeley's half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> in 1674 together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-byllynge">Edward Byllynge</a>. Disputes over the purchase, which came to be held in trust for Byllynge's creditors, led to the creation of West Jersey under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> and other trustees. In 1675 Fenwick led settlers to the Delaware and founded Salem, the first permanent English settlement in West Jersey.</p></div>
